  "full_response": "The perception that all politicians are liars can stem from several factors, not necessarily because they are inherently dishonest. Here are a few reasons this perception exists:\n\n1. **Nature of Politics**: Politics often involves complex issues and a multitude of stakeholders with different interests. Politicians sometimes must make promises or present information in a way that resonates with certain groups, which can lead to perceptions of dishonesty.\n\n2. **Spin and Rhetoric**: Politicians are skilled communicators and may use rhetoric to frame issues in the most favorable light. This can sometimes be perceived as dishonest or manipulative.\n\n3. **Dishonesty Exposure**: When politicians lie or are caught making false statements, these incidents are highly publicized. This can lead to a general perception that all politicians are untrustworthy.\n\n4. **Partisanship and Bias**: Political partisanship can lead people to view all members of the opposing party as dishonest, regardless of their actions. Media outlets may also highlight instances of dishonesty based on political bias.\n\n5. **Complex Situations**: Some promises or statements may become unpopular or impractical to implement, leading to broken promises without any intent to deceive originally.\n\n6. **Regulatory and Ethical Standards**: There are established ethical standards and regulations around truthfulness in politics, and when violators are caught, it reinforces the stereotype for some.\n\nIt's important to note that not all politicians are dishonest, and many are committed to serving the public with integrity. As with any profession, there are also conscientious and ethical individuals who prioritize honesty and transparency. Evaluating politicians on an individual basis and considering their actions over time can provide a more balanced view.",
